<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <LangVersion>preview</LangVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="Ticker.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Program.fs" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- No product dependency pulls FSharp.Core in as copy-local for a
         package-free console app; without this the built binary can't find
         it at run time even though it compiles fine. -->
    <PackageReference Include="FSharp.Core" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The counter below is held in a REGISTERED holder, which is what makes
         the app's live state visible to a hot-reload restart.

         A USER NEEDS NOTHING HERE. When SageFs builds this project — the Run
         App path, any session rebuild, a cold restart — it injects its own
         reference itself (`SessionBuild.prepareCoreReference` writes a
         .targets and passes -p:CustomAfterMicrosoftCommonTargets), so the
         holder API resolves with no Reference in this file. Proven end to end
         on a project with zero Reference elements.

         This Reference exists only for a RAW `dotnet build`, which is not a
         SageFs build and therefore gets no injection. It resolves through
         `$(SageFsCoreAssembly)`, which `Directory.Build.props` points at
         `SageFs.Core/bin/$(Configuration)/net10.0/`.

         THE GATE CAUGHT A REAL FAILURE HERE, and it is worth recording rather
         than just fixing. That path points at a BUILD OUTPUT, and a solution
         build has no ordering guarantee between projects — so the sample can
         compile against whatever `SageFs.Core.dll` happens to be on disk. In a
         clean checkout that is a STALE one, and the sample fails with
         "The value, constructor, namespace or type 'holdInCurrent' is not
         defined" while passing locally, where the DLL happens to be current.

         The honest fix is not a better path, it is to make the ORDERING
         explicit so this cannot compile against a stale assembly again. -->
    <Reference Include="SageFs.Core" Condition="'$(SageFsCoreAssembly)' != '' And '$(SageFsManagedBuild)' != 'true'">
      <HintPath>$(SageFsCoreAssembly)</HintPath>
      <Private>true</Private>
    </Reference>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Make the ordering explicit rather than relying on MSBuild's project
       traversal: the sample needs a Core that contains `holdInCurrent`, so it
       must not compile until Core has been rebuilt in this configuration.

       `Targets="Restore;Build"` because `Build` alone is not enough in a clean
       checkout: Core has no `obj/project.assets.json` there, so a Build-only
       invocation fails with

         error NETSDK1004: Assets file '…/SageFs.Core/obj/project.assets.json'
         not found. Run a NuGet package restore to generate this file.

       Restore is a SEPARATE task, not `Targets="Restore;Build"`. A semicolon in
       `Targets` means "run these", and MSBuild is free to run them in parallel —
       so Build starts before Restore has written the assets file and fails with

         error NETSDK1004: Assets file '…/SageFs.Core/obj/project.assets.json'
         not found.

       Two `<MSBuild>` tasks in sequence is what actually orders them, and
       restore is idempotent so the incremental case costs nothing.

       Passing only `Build` is what made this work locally (where assets exist)
       and fail on the gate (where they do not) — the same local-green/CI-red
       shape as the property condition this target was added to fix. Both halves
       of that trap had the same shape: something that only holds in a dirty
       checkout.

       This target and the Reference above are for a RAW `dotnet build` only.
       When SageFs builds the project (a hard reset, run_app) it injects its own
       Core reference and passes SageFsManagedBuild=true alongside it. The
       nested `<MSBuild>` below inherits every global property, so under SageFs
       it compiled Core WITH that injected reference to Core, every type in Core
       became ambiguous, and the rebuild failed without telling anyone. So both
       are skipped whenever SageFsManagedBuild is true. (Testing
       CustomAfterMicrosoftCommonTargets instead does not work: the SDK gives it a
       default of its own, so it is never empty.) The target is also skipped during
       a design-time load (Ionide evaluates the project that way), where a
       nested Restore and Build of Core made the loader return zero projects. -->
  <Target Name="BuildSageFsCoreFirst" BeforeTargets="ResolveAssemblyReferences" Condition="'$(SageFsCoreAssembly)' != '' And '$(DesignTimeBuild)' != 'true' And '$(SageFsManagedBuild)' != 'true'">
    <MSBuild Projects="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../../../SageFs.Core/SageFs.Core.fsproj"
             Targets="Restore"
             Properties="Configuration=$(Configuration)"
             BuildInParallel="false" />
    <MSBuild Projects="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../../../SageFs.Core/SageFs.Core.fsproj"
             Targets="Build"
             Properties="Configuration=$(Configuration)"
             BuildInParallel="false">
      <Output TaskParameter="TargetOutputs" ItemName="_SageFsCoreOutput" />
    </MSBuild>
  </Target>

</Project>
